caller at all; T099 scored the model path and the baseline in production rather
than only inside a test; T100 routed every run-level abort through the
constructor for its kind; T101 added FR-069's third resolution, the correct
negative; T102 published the printed fields with no vocabulary term; T103
narrowed the page-split contributor rule. All recorded in tasks.md Phase 8.

## Why the two blocked tasks cannot be closed here

Two independent causes, either of which alone is sufficient.

1. A fixture is a recorded provider response. None can be produced without
   network access and a provider credential, neither of which is available.

2. **No fixture could be replayed even if recorded.**
   `gateway.compute.hashing.fixture_key` digests `request.model_dump_json()`
   and `trace_id` is a declared field on `InvocationRequest` carrying no
   `exclude=True`, so the correlation identifier is part of the key. FR-070
   mints one trace id per run, so the same request keys differently on every
   run. Verified directly on the committed code: two requests differing only
   in trace id give sha256:667b82331c676441... and sha256:b32df59be116cbd7...
   This is E004's defect, it survived E004's QC because `resolve_trace_id`
   mints an id when the caller supplies none (so E004's own tests key with
   trace_id null and stay stable), and E006 is the first caller obliged to
   supply one. It is left unfixed because it belongs to a shipped, QC-passed
   epic and fixing it does not by itself unblock T081.

Two further E004 findings are recorded in tasks.md: `DEFAULT_FIXTURE_ROOT`
resolves relative to the installed package and lands inside `.venv` when
reached through the model entry, and the gateway rejects the
`postgresql+psycopg://` spelling that `ingest.writer` normalizes before use.

